            <ArticleTitle>Haemodynamic effects of a left stellate ganglion block in ASA I patients. An echocardiographic study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>79-84</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In anaesthetized dogs, stellate ganglion blockade led to a moderate disturbance in left ventricular diastolic function. We investigated the effect of a left-sided block, following injection of 10 mL bupivacaine 0.5%, on echocardiographic variables of ventricular function in eight otherwise healthy patients with sympathetically mediated pain syndromes. After the blockade, heart rate (control: 66+/-3 (mean+/- SEM), block: 64+/-3 min-1) and mean arterial blood pressure (88.5+/-6.0 vs. 84.0+/-8.1 mmHg) were unchanged, but afterload decreased (end-systolic meridional wall stress; 69.6+/-9.9 vs. 59.8+/-7.1, P &lt; 0.05). Stroke volume increased from 71.2+/-8.1 to 79.6+/-7.4 mL, P &lt; 0.05. Variables of systolic function were unchanged, but relaxation was prolonged (isovolumic relaxation time; 71+/-5 vs. 81+/-4 ms, P &lt; 0.05). In patients who were ASA I, there was a small impairment in echocardiographic variables during ventricular relaxation after a left stellate ganglion blockade. This small effect did not compromise ventricular function, and the heart responded with a small stroke volume increase to the simultaneous afterload reduction.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
